From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: bq256xx: reset the part before configuring it

The regmap is created with a full cache seeded from the static power-on
defaults, and everything except the input current limit and the status
block is cached:

	.reg_defaults	= bq2560x_reg_defs,
	.cache_type	= REGCACHE_FLAT,
	.volatile_reg	= bq256xx_is_volatile_reg,

so regmap_update_bits() decides whether a write is needed by comparing
against those defaults instead of against what the part actually holds.
The driver therefore assumes the charger is at power-on state when it
probes. That is usually true, but not always.

On a Motorola Moto G82 5G the Android bootloader leaves the charger with
charging disabled and the watchdog off, so the state survives into Linux.
The consequences are silent:

  - bq25601 defaults say CHG_CONFIG is set, so an update_bits() that tries
    to enable charging is dropped as a no-op and the part never charges.
    Writing "Fast" to charge_type appears to succeed and changes nothing,
    and reading it back returns "N/A" because the cache is untouched too.
  - the same applies to any register whose real content happens to differ
    from the assumed default.

Reset the part at the start of bq256xx_hw_init() so the cache tells the
truth, and enable charging explicitly rather than relying on the reset
having done it.

--- a/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
 #include <linux/usb/phy.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
 #include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
@@ -1563,6 +1564,29 @@
 	int ret = 0;
 	int i;
 
+	/*
+	 * The register cache is seeded from the static power-on defaults, and
+	 * every register except the input current limit and the status block
+	 * is cached, so regmap_update_bits() decides whether to write based on
+	 * those defaults rather than on what the part actually holds. If a
+	 * bootloader or a previous OS left the charger configured, the cache
+	 * lies and writes are silently dropped as no-ops.
+	 *
+	 * Put the part back to its power-on state so the cache tells the
+	 * truth. This also undoes any charging-disabled state left behind,
+	 * since CHG_CONFIG is set at power-on.
+	 */
+	ret = regmap_update_bits(bq->regmap, BQ256XX_PART_INFORMATION,
+				 BQ256XX_REG_RST, BQ256XX_REG_RST);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * The reset bit clears itself. No cache invalidation is needed: the
+	 * cache was seeded with the same power-on values the part now holds.
+	 */
+	usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < BQ256XX_NUM_WD_VAL; i++) {
 		if (bq->watchdog_timer == bq256xx_watchdog_time[i]) {
 			wd_reg_val = i;
@@ -1645,6 +1669,16 @@
 			return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Charging is enabled at power-on, but be explicit: the reset above is
+	 * the only thing keeping this true, and a part that comes up with
+	 * charging disabled would otherwise never charge.
+	 */
+	ret = bq->chip_info->bq256xx_set_charge_type(bq,
+						POWER_SUPPLY_CHARGE_TYPE_FAST);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	power_supply_put_battery_info(bq->charger, bat_info);
 
 	return 0;
